course: dictionary
lesson: 2241
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. «Cracker» is one of them, so you cannot pick a meaning until you read the words around it. These words are the context, and they leave clues.
explain: «The little holes in crackers let steam out so they bake flat.» Baked and holes tell you a cracker here is a thin, dry biscuit. Ask yourself: does my meaning fit the sentence? If it does, you are probably right.
explain: «At Christmas they had to sit next to each other with paper hats on, pulling a cracker.» Christmas and pulling are the clues. Here a cracker is a paper tube that bangs, with a small gift inside. A firecracker, by contrast, is a small firework, so the words around it would talk about a bang in the sky or a garden.
fact: A cracker can mean a thin, dry, crisp biscuit, often eaten with cheese.
fact: A Christmas cracker is a paper tube that bangs when two people pull it, with a small gift inside. It is used in the UK, Ireland and Australia.
fact: A firecracker is a small firework that bangs.
fact: Clue words show you the meaning: baked or cheese point to a biscuit, while Christmas or pulling point to a Christmas cracker.
fact: When a word has more than one meaning, swap each meaning into the sentence and keep the one that makes sense.
exercise: «The little holes in crackers let steam out so they bake flat.» What is a cracker in this sentence? | A paper tube with a gift inside | *A thin, dry, crisp biscuit | A small firework that bangs | A tall tower of bricks
exercise: «Kieran sat with a paper hat on, pulling a cracker.» What is a cracker in this sentence? | A thin, dry, crisp biscuit | A cake with cream | *A paper tube that bangs, with a small gift inside | A loud firework in the sky
exercise: A cracker went bang in the park last night. Which meaning fits? | A cake | *A small firework that bangs | A thin, dry biscuit | A paper tube with a gift inside
test: «Cheese and crackers» was the snack on the table. What does cracker mean here? | A cracker on a Christmas tree | *A thin, dry biscuit eaten with cheese | A small firework that bangs | A paper tube with a gift inside
test: Which word in «Biscuit dough is rolled into a sheet, cut with shapes, and baked in a tunnel oven» is a clue that crackers are food? | paper | *baked | firework | Christmas
exam: «At Christmas, we pulled a cracker and laughed at the joke inside.» What does cracker mean here? | A firework in the sky | A paper hat | A biscuit with cheese | *A paper tube that bangs, with a small gift inside
